{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And Moses said to Aaron: Take one jar.</b> It does not say 'and Moses took one jar and put there,' when it should have said so, since Aaron had not yet been chosen for the priesthood — and after this we find that Moses was the one who offered and burned incense first in the Tent of Meeting until the priesthood was given to Aaron." "<b>And it is possible</b> that the reason Aaron was commanded in this matter is to allude that the priests and Levites, the teachers of Torah who would descend from Aaron, would have their sustenance in abundance and not through toil like the eaters of the manna. As it is written (II Chronicles 31:4): ״ויאמר לעם ליושבי ירושלים לתת מנת הכהנים והלוים למען יחזקו בתורת ה׳״. And he mentioned first ״and lay it up before HaShem״ to make clear that the sustenance of the Sages of every generation is entrusted to HaShem, blessed be He, and it is laid up before HaShem. Therefore it says, ״and lay it up before HaShem, to be kept throughout your generations״ — for it is laid up and kept before HaShem, blessed be He, for all generations. And he needed to allude to this because the Torah is from the source of supernal wisdom, and ״wisdom gives life to those who have it״ — and it is the very source of blessing and true life, as it is written (Psalms 134:2): ״שאו ידיכם קודש וברכו את ה׳״. Therefore it is fitting that livelihood and food, which are the life of the body, be bound to the Torah, which is the life of the soul — and afterwards ״before the testimony,״ for he laid some of the manna to be kept before the testimony, as a sign and witness for generations, so that they might see the bread. And because of this he mentioned in between ״as HaShem commanded Moses,״ when Scripture should have said, ״and Aaron laid it up before the testimony to be kept, as HaShem commanded Moses״ — but he clarified that the sustenance of the masters of Torah is kept and laid up before HaShem, as HaShem commanded Moses at Mount Sinai when He said to him (Exodus 34:1 — ״פסל לך״), that the excess stone from hewing the tablets should be his — as the Sages, of blessed memory, interpreted: from there he became wealthy, and from there the Holy One, blessed be He, prepared his livelihood." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״Take one jar״ — this is one of the ten things that were created at twilight. And when it says ״a jar,״ I do not know what it was made of — whether silver or gold, or copper, iron, or lead. And on its own you may infer: a jar (צנצנת) is something that cools (צונן) and keeps cool what is inside it and preserves what is inside it — and it is of earth, that is, earthenware, as it says (Jeremiah 32:14): ״ונתתם בכלי חרש למען יעמדו ימים רבים״. And so too wine is not preserved except in an earthenware vessel, and this is what is written: ״and lay it up before HaShem, to be kept.״ And Jeremiah the prophet, peace be upon him, brought it out to Israel and said to them (Jeremiah 2:31): ״הדור אתם ראו דבר ה׳ המדבר הייתי לישראל אם ארץ מאפליה״. Jeremiah said to Israel: Why do you not engage in Torah? They said to him: If we engage in Torah, how will we be comfortable? How will we be sustained? At that moment he brought out to them the jar of manna and said to them: See — your fathers were in the wilderness and engaged in Torah; from where were they sustained? So too you, if you engage in Torah, the Holy One, blessed be He, will sustain you (ibid. v. 2): ״מדוע אמרו עמי רדנו לא נבא עוד עליך״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:25.226864128Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}